//! The SOS call numbers — KERNEL-INTERNAL (design 140; spec.md §5.7).
//!
//! Every op number, rights bit, status tag and handle type in the system, in
//! one place.
//! Exactly two things import this: the kernel's dispatch tables
//! (`kernel/core/`) and the `sos` wrapper module the kernel package exports
//! to userspace (`kernel/sysapi/`). Nothing else, ever.
//!
//! WHY IT IS SHARED RATHER THAN DUPLICATED: the dispatch and the wrappers are
//! the two halves of one contract. Held apart, they skew — and a syscall-number
//! skew is not a compile error anywhere, it is a working call that does the
//! wrong thing. Held together, renumbering is a one-line edit that either
//! rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! WHY IT IS KERNEL-INTERNAL: the Zircon-vDSO discipline (ratified Aug 5).
//! NUMBERS ARE NOT ABI. Userspace links the exported `sos` module and calls
//! methods; it never writes `7` for an op, so the kernel may renumber freely
//! between builds. The one thing a process must agree with the kernel about is
//! the CALLING CONVENTION (§5.7: handle in a0, op in a7), and that lives in the
//! per-arch HAL stub, not here.
//!
//! WHY THESE ARE ENUMS AND NOT STATICS (design 145/153). Every family here is a
//! CLOSED NAMED SET of values, which is what an Int-inheriting enum is for: it
//! names the set, PINS the numbers so a reorder cannot renumber them, gives the
//! dispatch an exhaustive `match`, and turns the read at the trap boundary into
//! `from(raw:)` — a total function returning `E?`, so an unrecognized number is
//! DATA the kernel rejects rather than an index it trusts. The parallel statics
//! this replaced (`OP_SYSTEM_DEBUG_PRINT`, `SYS_ERR_BAD_OP`, …) could be
//! compared against anything and validated only by a hand-written `> MAX` test.
//!
//! HOW A FAMILY IS NAMED (ruled Aug 15, user). An op table or a rights set is
//! named for the spec §2 OBJECT it belongs to, spelled OUT, plus the
//! term-of-art suffix §5.7 already uses: `SystemOp`, `ProcessOp`, `ThreadOp`,
//! `EventOp`, `WaiterOp`, `SystemRight`, `ProcessRight`, `ThreadRight`,
//! `EventRight`, `WaiterRight`. No abbreviated object prefix — not `SysOp`, not
//! `ProcRight`, not `EvtRight` — because the abbreviation saves four characters
//! in a file nobody types into and costs every reader the question of whether
//! `Sys` means the System object or the syscall. The rule extends to anything
//! else here named after an object (`ProcessStatusKind`, `process_status_word`,
//! `EventMode`, `WaiterReadiness`), and the next object kind inherits it: name
//! the family after the row in the §2 table.
//!
//! HOW AN OP IS NAMED (ruled Aug 17, user). Inside a family an op reads
//! `object_operation`: the OBJECT KIND the op concerns leads, then the verb —
//! `ProcessOp.ThreadCreate`, `SystemOp.ClockGet`, `ClockOp.TimerCreate` — and
//! the typed method and the C export mirror it (`process.thread_create`,
//! `sos_system_clock_get`). An op whose RECEIVER IS the object it concerns keeps
//! the bare verb, there being no second noun to lead with: `Arm`, `Ack`, `Wait`,
//! `Signal`, `Join`, `Start`, `Exit`, `Yield`, `Now`, `Add`, `Remove`,
//! `Shutdown`, `DebugPrint`, `GetStatus`. What the rule buys is that every op
//! concerning a kind sorts together — a reader looking for what makes a Timer
//! finds `TimerCreate` without having to know which object is the factory — and
//! that a rights bit named after an op (`ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`) reads
//! exactly like the op it gates. A right naming a CAPABILITY rather than an op
//! (`Transfer`, `Mint`, `Read`, `Control`, `Attach`, `Debug`) is not under this
//! rule and keeps its own noun.
//!
//! Delivery in v1 is static linking through the manifest/module-path
//! mechanism. A genuinely mapped vDSO is an object-model-era upgrade that
//! changes none of the above.
//!
//! # THIS FILE IS THE FACADE (sawos design 26)
//!
//! The module used to be one 3,735-line file. It is now six leaf modules and
//! this facade, and this file holds NO DECLARATION: it re-exports what the
//! leaves declare, so every `import sosabi.{...}` in `kernel/core/`,
//! `kernel/sysapi/` and `tests/` means exactly what it always did — and so does
//! the `sosabi.` QUALIFIER, which `kernel/sysapi/src/system.saw` uses to reach
//! `ENTRY_IMAGE` without binding it bare.
//!
//! THE ONE-PLACE DOCTRINE IS UNCHANGED, and the split is what the doctrine
//! never said: it is ONE PACKAGE and ONE IMPORTABLE NAME that keep the dispatch
//! and the wrappers from skewing, not one FILE. Renumbering is still a one-line
//! edit that either rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! THE MODULES, IN DEPENDENCY ORDER — a file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is a compile error naming the loop (DF-232e):
//!
//!   `types`    what a handle-table entry names, the per-kind handle aliases,
//!              `NO_HANDLE`, the handle word's encoding and `ENTRY_IMAGE`
//!   `rights`   the fifteen `*Right` enums with the asserts that pin the
//!              universal low byte, the two kind-blind predicates, and what
//!              every default set mints
//!   `ops`      the two universal ops and every per-object op table — plus the
//!              argument encodings that sit under the last op-table banner
//!              (`ClockType`, `MapAccess`, `EventMode`, `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`,
//!              `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES`, `PipeMsgMeta`)
//!   `records`  the wire records both halves copy: the wait record and its
//!              levels, the Clock/Timer records, the pipe records and the
//!              process-stats record
//!   `boot`     the boot-handle record and the boot handle set
//!   `status`   the status words, the fault reasons and the §8 process status
//!
//! EXACTLY ONE EDGE CROSSES A LEAF BOUNDARY: `records` imports
//! `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`, `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` and `PipeMsgMeta` from `ops`, because
//! `WaitBuffer` and `PipeMsgRecord` are sized in them. It is one-way — nothing
//! in `ops` names a record — so nothing here is a cycle, and `records.saw`
//! carries the note at the import.
//!
//! WHY EVERY NAME IS LISTED, rather than one `public import sosabi.<leaf>.*`
//! per leaf. A GLOB DOES NOT CARRY A `type` ALIAS (SL-20, probed against sawc
//! 0.4.0 @ `46eebb36`): `public import sosabi.types.*` re-exports the statics,
//! the enums and the functions and silently omits the fifteen handle aliases,
//! so `MemoryHandle` became a name-only type in `kernel/sysapi/src/memory.saw`
//! — no constructor, and no longer flowing to `UInt` — in a file this split
//! never edited. The selective form carries an alias WHOLE, in every position
//! (construction, annotation, field, parameter, return), which is what these
//! lists are. They are GENERATED from the leaves' public declarations rather
//! than curated, so the surface is exactly the one file's: this is a zero-churn
//! split, not a re-decided API.
//!
//! So a declaration added to a leaf owes a name on the matching line here. That
//! is the same bargain `kcore`'s facade and `sos`'s already make, and here it
//! fails LOUDLY — an op number nothing republishes is unreachable at its first
//! use, not silently absent.
//!
//! WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the vDSO wall. `kernel/sysapi/src/lib.saw` names the
//! handful of encodings a process is meant to write and re-exports THOSE; the
//! op numbers, the rights bits and the handle aliases stay kernel-internal
//! because nothing republishes them, exactly as before.

public import sosabi.types.{ObjType, SystemHandle, ProcessHandle, ThreadHandle,
                            EventHandle, WaiterHandle, InterruptHandle,
                            ClockHandle, TimerHandle, MemoryHandle,
                            IoMemoryHandle, MappingHandle, PipeInletHandle,
                            PipeOutletHandle, PipeReplyHandle,
                            PipeRequestHandle, NO_HANDLE, HANDLE_WORD_BITS,
                            HANDLE_INDEX_BITS, HANDLE_GENERATION_BITS,
                            HANDLE_INDEX_MASK, HANDLE_GENERATION_MODULUS,
                            ENTRY_IMAGE}

public import sosabi.rights.{SystemRight, ProcessRight, ThreadRight,
                             EventRight, WaiterRight, InterruptRight,
                             ClockRight, TimerRight, MemoryRight,
                             IoMemoryRight, MappingRight, PipeInletRight,
                             PipeOutletRight, PipeReplyRight, PipeRequestRight,
                             rights_allow_transfer, rights_allow_mint,
                             root_system_rights, process_rights,
                             pipe_inlet_rights, pipe_outlet_rights,
                             pipe_reply_rights, pipe_request_rights,
                             memory_rights, iomemory_rights, mapping_rights,
                             thread_rights, event_rights, waiter_rights,
                             interrupt_rights, clock_rights, timer_rights}

public import sosabi.ops.{RELEASE_OP, MINT_OP, SystemOp, ProcessOp,
                          BootTagForm, ThreadOp, EventOp, WaiterOp,
                          WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT, WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING,
                          WAITER_ADD_FLAGS, InterruptOp, ClockOp, TimerOp,
                          MemoryOp, IoMemoryOp, MappingOp, PipeInletOp,
                          PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyOp, PipeRequestOp,
                          ReplyWaitLeg, ClockType, MapAccess, MAP_ACCESS_MASK,
                          EventMode, SlabKind, ProtectionTier, PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
                          PIPE_MSG_HANDLES, PipeMsgMeta}

public import sosabi.records.{WaitTag, WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD,
                              WAIT_TAG_WORD, WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD,
                              WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS, WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT,
                              WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0, WaitBuffer, wait_header_bytes,
                              wait_meta_end_bytes, wait_prefix_bytes,
                              wait_record_bytes, OutletLevel, InletLevel,
                              ReplyLevel, CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT, clock_now_record_bytes,
                              TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT, TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT,
                              timer_arm_record_bytes, PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS,
                              PIPE_CREATE_INLET_WORD, PIPE_CREATE_OUTLET_WORD,
                              pipe_create_record_bytes, PipeMsgRecord,
                              PipeTakeRecord, pipe_msg_record_bytes,
                              pipe_msg_body_offset, pipe_take_record_bytes,
                              pipe_take_body_offset, PipeSendRecord,
                              pipe_send_record_bytes, PipeReplyWaitRecord,
                              pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes,
                              PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS,
                              PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT,
                              PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT,
                              PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT,
                              process_stats_record_bytes}

public import sosabi.boot.{BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS, BOOT_HANDLE_TAG_WORD,
                           BOOT_HANDLE_KIND_WORD, BOOT_HANDLE_HANDLE_WORD,
                           boot_handle_record_bytes, BootHandleKind,
                           ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE, NO_USER_STACK}

public import sosabi.status.{SosStatus, FaultReason, ProcessStatusKind,
                             process_status_word}
